WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.000
What we're doing is trying to take mutations
in different genes and combine them together

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.929
to understand how genes work together to coordinate
cell life.

00:00:08.929 --> 00:00:12.679
And we do this in a simple organism like yeast
that only has 6,000 genes.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:18.300
But remarkably yeast is very similar to humans
so it provides a very good model to understand.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.730
What we learn in yeast can provide us with
some direction or a reference to understand

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:28.439
— to help us understand how genes work together
to help modulate central functions and life

00:00:28.439 --> 00:00:29.439
in human cells.

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:34.180
So what we do is take a collection of yeast
strains, each of which is deleted for a different

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.420
gene or mutated for a different gene, and
we use a technology we developed in our lab

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:42.730
that allows us to combine pairs of mutations
in the same cell.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:47.260
And then we can simply ask: Do the mutations
in these pairs of genes allow the cell to

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:48.540
live or not?

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:54.480
And we use robotic technologies to do this
and we were able to test all 18 million possible

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.960
mutant combinations.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.580
And we don't think that genes work in isolation.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.770
So there are obviously many examples of Mendelian
diseases where a single cell mutation in a

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.110
single gene results in a disease.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.500
So those cases would be highly predictive.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:15.750
We think that many common diseases are affected
by combinations of mutations, rare mutations

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:16.750
in different genes.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:21.700
What we've done is tested all possible double
mutant combinations to build this large network.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.310
And we see many structures in these networks
that we think can provide a reference from

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.750
which we start to learn rules and principles
that might help us to understand or simplify

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:38.280
the problem in humans and help us develop
rules for how different genes interact.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:44.760
We believe this will hep us simplify the problem
or at least provide a guide to start to understand

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.130
how genes work together to modify complex
disease in humans.

